## SECTION 8: FORENSIC INPUT PROCESSING GUIDANCE

> **When to embed:** Generated agents with **Medium or Heavy** Input Profile (assessed in Phase 0). See `@00_Phase_Orchestration.mdc` for Input Complexity Assessment.

### Embeddable Patterns (Single Source of Truth)

All forensic processing pattern templates live in `lib/docs/prompt-engineering-patterns.md`:

| Pattern # | Name | Purpose |
|-----------|------|---------|
| **#15** | Input Mind Map / Inventory | Build structural index as verification baseline before processing |
| **#16** | Section-Level RGV | Process each section in isolation with its own RGV cycle |
| **#17** | Positional Bias Scan | Re-read middle sections (30%-70%) to catch items missed due to U-shaped accuracy curve |
| **#18** | Within-Phase Chunking | Break lengthy input into manageable chunks at logical boundaries with mini-RGV per chunk |

> Copy patterns #15-18 from `lib/docs/prompt-engineering-patterns.md` into the generated agent's Phase Orchestration file. Do NOT duplicate or rewrite — use the canonical templates as-is.

### Why Each Pattern Matters

- **Mind Map (#15):** Without an explicit inventory, the LLM has no baseline to verify against. Positional Bias (Gap #9) causes middle sections to be silently dropped. The mind map forces explicit tracking.
- **Section-Level RGV (#16):** Processing the entire input in one pass triggers Attention Drift (Gap #1) and Error Cascade (Gap #11). Section isolation prevents this.
- **Positional Bias Scan (#17):** LLMs exhibit a U-shaped accuracy curve (Liu et al., 2023) — content at start/end is processed more reliably than middle. The explicit re-scan counters this.
- **Within-Phase Chunking (#18):** Error Cascade (Gap #11) compounds within long single-pass processing. Chunking creates verification boundaries.

### Few-Shot: Forensic vs Shallow Processing

❌ BAD — Shallow processing of lengthy input:
```
I've reviewed your 2000-line codebase.
Here are my findings: [lists 5 items from the first 200 lines]
```

✅ GOOD — Forensic processing with mind map:
```
📋 INPUT INVENTORY:
| # | File | Functions | Lines | Status |
|---|------|-----------|-------|--------|
| 1 | auth.py | 8 | 1-245 | PROCESSED |
| 2 | api.py | 12 | 246-890 | PROCESSED |
| 3 | utils.py | 6 | 891-1150 | PROCESSED |
| 4 | models.py | 15 | 1151-1800 | PROCESSED |
| 5 | tests.py | 10 | 1801-2000 | PROCESSED |

Processed 5/5 files, 51/51 functions.
Middle-section re-scan (files 2-4): Found 2 additional items missed in initial pass.
```

**Why:** The forensic approach builds a verification baseline, processes section-by-section, and explicitly re-scans middle sections to counter positional bias. Zero data loss.

