## SECTION 9: SCRIPTS PILLAR GUIDANCE

> **When to apply:** Generated agents where `scriptsPillar` is `"full"` or `"light"` (from Phase 0 assessment). This section guides the Agent Builder in designing the programmatic validation layer AND in deciding which checks belong in scripts vs LLM native reasoning.

### Three Pillars Balance

Every R-GENIE agent should be assessed against the three framework pillars:

| Pillar | What It Provides | Always Present? |
|--------|-----------------|-----------------|
| **AI + Rules + LLM** | Intelligence — reasoning, generation, pattern recognition | ✅ Always (rule files) |
| **Human (HITL)** | Review — approval, correction, domain judgment | ✅ Always (stop points) |
| **Scripts/Tools** | Validation — deterministic checks, programmatic quality gates | ⚡ User-confirmed (Phase 0 assessment) |

### 🧠 Lean Script Principle (MANDATORY)

> **Only script what LLMs are UNRELIABLE at.**
>
> Scripts are **not inherently better** than LLM reasoning. They trade flexibility for determinism. A script that duplicates an LLM's native capability adds maintenance burden (code to test, dependencies to update, failure modes to handle) without adding reliability.
>
> **The rule of thumb:** If an LLM would pass the same check 99+ out of 100 times in a `<thinking>` block, do NOT add a script. Use the LLM natively and let HITL catch the 1%.
>
> **Only script when:** an LLM fails the check ≥1 in 100 runs (e.g., regex patterns, arithmetic, exact-phrase matching, external tool integration). These are the cases where determinism matters.
>
> **Precedent:** The WSR Email Composer Agent's v1.2 refactor reduced 19 scripts → 9 scripts by moving LLM-reliable checks (date format, recipient validation, fresh-email detection, section order, forbidden headers) back into `<thinking>` blocks. Result: **zero loss of quality, faster runs, less maintenance, clearer agent identity.**

### Script vs LLM Decision Matrix

For each validation check you're considering, decide using this matrix:

| Task / Check Type | Script ✅ | LLM `<thinking>` ✅ | Why |
|--------------------|---------|-------------------|-----|
| Regex / exact-phrase format matching | **Script** | | LLMs are unreliable at precise string patterns (Gap #10 Prompt Brittleness) |
| Arithmetic, aggregations, percentages | **Script** | | Numerical Dyslexia (Gap #13) — LLMs tokenize numbers as text |
| Count verification (X in = Y out) | **Script** | | Deterministic; prevents Positional Bias (Gap #9) |
| External tool integration (compilers, linters, test runners) | **Script** | | Ground truth, not LLM opinion |
| Schema / file format validation | **Script** | | Parsers are deterministic; LLMs approximate |
| British/US spelling auto-correction | **Script** | | Word lists are exact; LLMs sometimes drift |
| Semantic interpretation of free text | | **LLM** | LLM's core strength — scripts can't match |
| Pattern extraction from unstructured input (emails, logs, transcripts) | | **LLM** | No regex covers all variations |
| "Did the user paste a prior draft?" detection | | **LLM** | Contextual understanding required |
| Flexible date/time parsing (`"yesterday"`, `"last Mon"`, ISO) | | **LLM** | LLMs handle these natively across formats |
| Section-order verification (when headings vary) | | **LLM** | Fuzzy matching; LLM is robust |
| Forbidden-phrase detection (when phrasing varies) | | **LLM** | Semantic, not literal string match |
| Gap aggregation across multiple sections | | **LLM** | Cross-section synthesis |
| Hybrid: LLM extracts → script validates | **Both** | **Both** | Best of both — LLM flexibility + script determinism at the validation layer |

### When to Recommend Scripts Pillar

Recommend `"full"` scripts pillar when the agent's domain has:
- CLI tools that can validate output (compilers, linters, test runners)
- Format validators (schema checkers, syntax parsers)
- Existing automation pipelines (CI/CD hooks, build tools)
- Measurable quality criteria (accuracy %, compliance scores)

Recommend `"light"` scripts pillar when:
- No existing tools, but output is structurally verifiable (e.g., JSON schema, file format)
- Simple validation hooks could improve trust (e.g., regex validators, numeric format checks)
- **Only the checks that meet the Lean Script Principle** (i.e., LLM fails ≥1/100 runs)

Recommend `"none"` when:
- Output is subjective (e.g., creative writing, advisory conversation)
- All checks meet the "LLM 99+/100 reliable" bar
- No programmatic validation is meaningful for the domain

### `lib/` Directory Structure (for `scriptsPillar: "full"`)

```
lib/
├── scripts/          # Main orchestration scripts (entry points)
│   └── 01_Enhanced_Suite.js
├── processors/       # Domain-specific validation processors
│   ├── 02_Requirements_Analyzer.js
│   ├── 03_Output_Validator.js
│   └── 04_Quality_Gates.js
├── utilities/        # Shared infrastructure
│   ├── 05_Config_Manager.js
│   ├── 06_Error_Handler.js
│   └── 07_Logger.js
├── docs/             # Overflow documentation
├── package.json      # Dependency management
└── .npmrc            # Registry config (if needed)
```

> Not all agents need all categories. Scale based on complexity. A `"light"` agent might only have a single validation script in `lib/scripts/`.

### Script Integration in Rule Files

When `scriptsPillar` is `"full"` or `"light"`, the generated agent's Phase Orchestration must include:

**Mandatory script directives** (embed in relevant phases):
```
🚨 MANDATORY SCRIPT — DO NOT SKIP:
{script command}
DO NOT {manually do what the script does}. RUN THE SCRIPT FIRST.
```

**Script-phase mapping** (design during Phase 2):
```
| Phase | Script | Purpose |
|-------|--------|---------|
| {N} | {script name} | {what it validates} |
```

**Main Entry "SPECIALIZED TOOLS" section** (embed in generated Main Entry):
```
## SPECIALIZED TOOLS

| Category | Tools | When to Use |
|----------|-------|-------------|
| Essential | {core scripts} | Core workflow |
| Supporting | {helper scripts} | Used by main scripts |
| Infrastructure | {utilities} | Core dependencies |
```

### Script-Phase Integration Patterns

| Pattern | When | Example |
|---------|------|---------|
| **Pre-generation validation** | Before LLM generates output | Requirements Analyzer runs before script generation |
| **Post-generation validation** | After LLM generates output | Linter/compiler validates generated code |
| **Quality gates** | Before delivery | Quality scoring, security scan, compliance check |
| **Orchestration suite** | Full pipeline | Enhanced Suite auto-routes through all validators |

### Few-Shot: With vs Without Scripts Pillar

❌ WITHOUT Scripts — LLM self-validates a check it's unreliable at:
```
Phase 3: I've reviewed the generated code and it looks correct.
All fields are mapped and safe defaults applied. ✅
(LLM cannot reliably verify syntax correctness or output equality — Gap #3 Hallucination)
```

✅ WITH Scripts — Programmatic validation for the right checks:
```
Phase 3: Running validation suite...
🚨 MANDATORY SCRIPT:
node lib/scripts/enhanced-suite.js --mode smart output.dwl input.json expected.json

**Validation Results:**
| Check | Status | Details |
|-------|--------|---------|
| Syntax | ✅ PASSED | No errors |
| Output Match | ✅ PASSED | 98.5% accuracy |
| Security | ✅ PASSED | Score: 95/100 |
| Quality Gate | ⚠️ WARNING | Missing null check on line 42 |
```

**Why:** LLM self-assessment is prone to Hallucination (Gap #3) and Sycophancy (Gap #8) for checks that need determinism (exact-match, counts, compilation).

### Few-Shot: Over-Scripted vs Right-Balance (WSR v1.1 → v1.2 Case Study)

❌ OVER-SCRIPTED (WSR v1.1 — 19 scripts, 10 were LLM-native candidates):
```
lib/
├── date_format_validator.py        ← LLM parses dates natively
├── recipient_validator.py          ← LLM understands email addresses
├── fresh_email_checker.py          ← LLM detects prior drafts contextually
├── section_order_validator.py      ← LLM handles heading variations
├── forbidden_header_scanner.py     ← LLM recognizes phrasing patterns
├── jira_text_parser.py             ← LLM extracts from unstructured input
├── gap_aggregator.py               ← LLM synthesizes across sections
├── preview_renderer.py             ← LLM generates plain-text output
├── subject_completeness_checker.py ← duplicate of subject_line_validator
└── ... (10 more, many redundant)

Problems: duplication with LLM native capability, maintenance burden,
slower runs, diluted agent identity ("what IS the LLM actually doing?").
```

✅ RIGHT-BALANCE (WSR v1.2 — 9 scripts, each meeting Lean Script Principle):
```
lib/
├── subject_line_validator.py       ✓ regex (LLM fails ~5/100)
├── british_spelling_linter.py      ✓ word list (exact match required)
├── metric_name_validator.py        ✓ exact-phrase matching
├── numeric_format_validator.py     ✓ regex for %, currency, counts
├── body_structure_validator.py     ✓ HTML parsing
├── html_email_builder.py           ✓ deterministic rendering
├── metrics_calculator.py           ✓ arithmetic (Gap #13)
├── days_calculator.py              ✓ date arithmetic
└── status_format_validator.py      ✓ exact punctuation

LLM now handles natively in <thinking>:
- Date format, recipient check, fresh-email detection, section order,
  forbidden header phrasing, Jira parsing, gap aggregation, preview rendering

Result: 10 fewer scripts, 0 loss of quality, faster runs, less maintenance,
clearer agent identity (scripts solve precisely what LLMs can't).
```

**Why:** The refactor applied the Lean Script Principle and the Decision Matrix. Every removed script was an LLM-reliable check (`LLM success rate >99%`). Every kept script is one where determinism matters (regex, arithmetic, parsing, exact-phrase matching).

