## SECTION 10: PROGRESSIVE DOCUMENTATION v2 — TEMPLATE-FIRST / PER-PHASE WRITE CONTRACT

> **When to apply (v2 — DEFAULT, not conditional):** ANY generated agent producing a structured deliverable across multiple phases — design docs, analyses, reports, configs, panel decks, advisory artifacts. STRICTLY MANDATORY when `longFormOutput: true` (deliverable >300 lines). Strongly recommended for shorter multi-phase deliverables (e.g., 150-line HLD across 5 phases). Set `progressiveDocV2: true` in state file when this pattern applies. See `lib/docs/prompt-engineering-patterns.md` Pattern #20 v2 for the canonical embeddable template.
>
> **Why v2 supersedes v1 "long-form only":** Even a 200-line HLD built across 6 phases hits the same failure modes (positional bias, mid-conversation truncation, context exhaustion) when all content is held in chat until a single final write. The CSA v1.7.0 reform (2026-05-12) proved that per-phase persistence beats single-phase generation regardless of total deliverable size. See `r-genie/Cloud_Success_Architect_Agent/Production_Learnings.md` Learning #1.1.

### Why Progressive (v2 — Five Failure Modes)

AI IDE edit tools (Cursor, Windsurf, Cline) and the underlying LLM are subject to five compounding failure modes that progressive writes mitigate:

1. **Truncation risk** — output may silently cut off near token limits
2. **Error cascade (Gap #11)** — per-token error rate compounds over longer outputs
3. **Positional bias (Gap #9)** — mid-section content (30-70% position) is systematically more likely to be dropped
4. **Structural invariant breakage** — YAML frontmatter, code fences, Markdown tables corrupt more often in long generations
5. **NEW in v2: Context window exhaustion mid-run** — when all phases hold content only in chat until a single final write, mid-run context loss causes the final write to lose earlier-phase decisions (the original CSA v1.6.0 healthcare-recommendations failure mode)

### 10.1 — The Five Mechanics

**Mechanic 1 — Template-First Skeleton (Phase 0 of the generated agent)**

The generated agent's Phase 0 MUST create the deliverable file(s) with:
- All planned section headings (one per planned section)
- Visible placeholder markers (e.g., `_{pending Phase N}_` or `<!-- SECTION: section-name -->`)
- Front-matter/metadata block
- **Zero analytical content** — only structure

Placeholders double as `old_string` anchors for targeted `edit` operations in later phases — make them unique and greppable.

**Mechanic 2 — Per-Phase Write Contract**

Each phase N (N ≥ 1) of the generated agent MUST write its approved phase output to the corresponding section of the deliverable file **before** its user checkpoint. The agent's Phase Orchestration file MUST carry an explicit Phase → Section mapping table.

**Mechanic 3 — Preview-in-Chat → Approve → Write-Clean-to-File**

For each phase producing analytical content:
1. Preview the substance in chat (compact, bullet-style or table)
2. STOP and WAIT for user approval / correction
3. After approval, write the clean prose to the section marker via `edit`/`multi_edit` (pre-read first)
4. Show a 3-5 line confirmation in chat (section name, line count, file path) — never re-dump full section prose

This loop separates "did we agree on the substance?" (cheap chat) from "is the artifact updated?" (file write). It keeps chat noise low and preserves context window for later phases.

**Mechanic 4 — Phase N Consolidate-and-Polish Mode**

The agent's final-output phase is redefined as **Consolidate & Polish**, NOT "from-scratch generation":
- Re-read the persisted file
- Apply cross-section consistency edits (executive summary tightening, terminology alignment, KPI/diagram cross-references, intro/conclusion polishing)
- Verify completeness of each section against the template
- Replace any residual placeholders left by skipped phases
- Run final style/length self-check against the example anchor (see §10.5)

The consolidate phase MUST NOT regenerate prose already approved and persisted in earlier phases.

**Mechanic 5 — What Stays in Chat vs File**

| Type | Goes to file | Stays in chat only |
|------|--------------|---------------------|
| Approved section prose | ✅ File | — |
| Diagrams (Mermaid blocks) | ✅ File (or sibling `_diagrams.md`) | — |
| Research log, citations, evidence index | ✅ File (`research_log.md` or Supporting Doc) | — |
| Brainstorm options, rejected branches | ✅ File (Supporting Doc or Refinement Log) | Summary table only |
| Phase confirmation, approvals | — | ✅ Chat |
| Compliance self-check results | — | ✅ Chat (pass/fail table) |

### 10.2 — Skeleton Placeholder Convention

```markdown
## 3. Architecture

_{pending Phase 2}_

<!-- SECTION: architecture — Will contain pattern selection, flow diagram,
     connector inventory. Written in Phase 2 after user approves the proposal. -->

## 4. Mappings

_{pending Phase 3}_

<!-- SECTION: mappings — Written in Phase 3 after design approval. -->
```

Rules for placeholders:
- Each placeholder has a **unique** section ID (`architecture`, `mappings`, `kpis`, etc.) — never reuse
- Each placeholder is **greppable** (`<!-- SECTION:` consistent prefix) for the orchestration code to locate
- Each placeholder includes a **brief inline description** of what will populate it and which phase writes it

### 10.3 — When to Apply (Decision Tree)

```
Does the agent produce a structured deliverable (markdown doc, multi-section report, config bundle)?
├── NO  → Skip Pattern #20 v2. Use standard single-phase generation.
└── YES → Is the deliverable produced across multiple phases (≥3 generation phases)?
         ├── NO  → Optional. Use Pattern #20 v2 if deliverable >150 lines (template-first only).
         └── YES → MANDATORY. Apply all 5 mechanics. Set `progressiveDocV2: true` in state file.
                  └── Is the deliverable >300 lines?
                       └── YES → ALSO set `longFormOutput: true` (v1 trigger preserved for compatibility).
```

### 10.4 — Per-Phase Write Contract Template (Embed in Generated Agent's Phase Orchestration)

```markdown
## PROGRESSIVE DOCUMENTATION v2 — PER-PHASE WRITE CONTRACT

The {primary deliverable filename} is built across all generation phases. Each phase writes to specific sections before its checkpoint:

| Phase | Section(s) Written | Marker(s) | Mode |
|-------|-------------------|-----------|------|
| **0** | Skeleton creation: all section headings + placeholders | All `_{pending}_` markers | CREATE |
| **1** | §1 {first section} | `<!-- SECTION: first-section -->` | REPLACE |
| **2** | §2 {second section} | `<!-- SECTION: second-section -->` | REPLACE |
| **...** | ... | ... | REPLACE |
| **N-1** | §{N-1} {penultimate section} | `<!-- SECTION: ... -->` | REPLACE |
| **N (final)** | Cross-cutting polish across all sections | (multiple) | CONSOLIDATE — NOT regenerate |

**Per-phase task block (insert into every phase):**
```
**Artifact Writes (Phase {N} — Progressive Documentation v2):**
- After user approves the Phase {N} preview, write the approved content to `<!-- SECTION: {marker} -->` in `{deliverable file}`
- Show 3-5 line file-write confirmation in chat (section name, line count, file path) — do NOT re-dump prose
- Run the Per-Phase Write Self-Check (see Stop Points file) before presenting the Phase {N} checkpoint
```

### 10.5 — Example-Anchored Conciseness (replaces hard line caps)

Generated agents that produce structured deliverables MUST anchor verbosity discipline against a **canonical example output**, not against hard line caps. Hard caps create the wrong incentive (truncate substance to hit a number); example anchoring drives **structural budgets** (sentence counts per executive summary, table-only sections, K-word quote rules, etc.).

**Pattern:**
- Embed one or two example outputs under `examples/` (or `lib/docs/examples/`)
- Reference them in the agent's Guidance Quality Checklist: "Your output should match the conciseness profile of `{example file}` — bullet structure, table density, sentence length per section"
- Replace any "must be ≤ N lines" rule with "benchmark length against `{example file}` (which lands at ~{X} lines for this complexity tier)"

**Precedent:** CSA v1.7.0 retired the hard 250-line HLD cap and replaced it with example-anchored conciseness based on `retail-personalized-offerings_HLD.md` (190 lines for medium complexity). See CSA `Production_Learnings.md` Learning #1.2.

### Canonical Reference Agents

- **`r-genie/01_Technical_Design_Agent/rules/01-01_Phase_Orchestration.mdc`** — Long-form precedent (600-1000 line documents, 6 phases)

| Phase | What It Writes | Edit Size |
|-------|----------------|-----------|
| **Phase 0** | Skeleton with section placeholders | ~50-80 lines |
| **Phase 1** | Project Overview section | ~80-150 lines |
| **Phase 2** | Architecture + Sequence diagram sections | ~150-250 lines |
| **Phase 3** | Mappings + Error Handling + Connector diagrams | ~200-300 lines |
| **Phase 4** | Document completion verification | N/A |
| **Phase 5** | Supplementary sections on request | ~100-250 lines each |

- **`r-genie/Cloud_Success_Architect_Agent/rules/00_Phase_Orchestration.mdc` (v1.7.0)** — Medium-form precedent (190-300 line HLDs, 9 phases, all-phases write contract)

### Per-Edit Size Budget

| Budget | When |
|--------|------|
| **≤300 lines per `edit`** | Standard target |
| **≤500 lines** | Absolute ceiling — only for structurally indivisible sections (one large Mermaid diagram) |
| **Split further** | If content > 300 lines, multiple `edit` calls within the same phase (pre-read each time) |

### Few-Shot: Wait-Until-Final-Phase vs Progressive v2

❌ WAIT-UNTIL-FINAL-PHASE (CSA v1.6.0 healthcare run, root cause of v1.7.0 reform):
```
Phase 0: Use case ingested (chat only).
Phase 1: Pain themes discussed (chat only).
Phase 2: Capabilities explored (chat only).
Phase 3: Solution brainstormed (chat only).
Phase 4: Decision locked (chat only).
Phase 5: "Now I'll write the HLD..." [context window 87% exhausted]
[HLD generation truncates mid-architecture section]
[User abandons run; restarts]
```

✅ PROGRESSIVE v2 (CSA v1.7.0):
```
Phase 0: Created `{slug}_HLD.md` + `{slug}_Supporting.md` skeletons (8 section placeholders each).
Phase 1: Wrote §1 Executive Summary + §2 Inputs to HLD. STOP — review.
Phase 2: Wrote §3 Pain Themes to HLD; appended research notes to Supporting. STOP — review.
Phase 3: Wrote §4 Capabilities to HLD + research_log.md. STOP — review.
Phase 4: Wrote §5 Solution Branches to Supporting; §6 Decision Log to HLD. STOP — review.
Phase 5: Wrote §7 Data Flows to HLD; generated `{slug}_diagrams.md`. STOP — review.
Phase 6 (Consolidate): Polished executive summary, cross-referenced KPIs, ran style benchmark. STOP — review.
Phase 7: Wrote KPI baselines, Q&A bank to Supporting. Final.
```

**Why:** Every phase persists its approved content. Context window stays clean. The final phase is polish, not generation. If a run is interrupted at Phase 4, the file already has §1-§4 fully written.

### Compliance Checklist for Progressive Documentation v2 Agents

- [ ] `progressiveDocV2: true` set in state file (Phase 0)
- [ ] Pattern #20 v2 Per-Phase Write Contract embedded in Phase Orchestration
- [ ] Phase 0 creates deliverable file(s) skeleton with named placeholders (not blank file)
- [ ] Each generation phase writes ONE bounded section group (≤300 lines per edit)
- [ ] Each phase has an explicit "Artifact Writes (Phase N):" task block
- [ ] User checkpoint between each section write (preview → approve → write loop)
- [ ] Final phase is in CONSOLIDATE mode (not from-scratch generation)
- [ ] Stop Points file includes the Per-Phase Write Self-Check
- [ ] Critical Rule #6 + #7 referenced in Main Entry mandatory behavior
- [ ] One canonical example output present under `examples/` for conciseness anchoring (§10.5)

